#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068564 :question/id #uuid "e2579380-1933-5819-a39e-9ffcfdafeeee" :question/math-academy-id "e-452" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $6x^2$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}(2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2x^3+3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{(2x^3+3)^7}{7}+C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628 :topic/math-academy-id 334 :topic/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2452d0d5-3314-543c-8155-cd466478eb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{6x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288264} {:answer/id #uuid "5181e9aa-208f-5201-b3ec-4362a8aec6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288598} {:answer/id #uuid "618b6980-5d49-54b2-98e1-6b2c17a106f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288715} {:answer/id #uuid "c874aedd-75e1-5980-8701-168e514e7383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289359} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9cf091-1a90-540f-a2e6-02899e4f742d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c874aedd-75e1-5980-8701-168e514e7383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289359} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6d7df2-4fc8-500a-b204-7a34c353e466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289923}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "082a63d7-3326-5dca-8bbb-73a2f7476913" :question/math-academy-id "q-2189" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $3{x}^{2}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{3}+4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5927e7-3d1a-5589-9ab4-b01b479f66f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288882} {:answer/id #uuid "a65798db-1640-5ac0-9550-f0316b86d818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289133} {:answer/id #uuid "a71c627c-e9b2-5a11-b21d-b190f3a77006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289138} {:answer/id #uuid "d436ea16-40f5-50d5-8944-8947c0a63c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289436} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4463e3-17c4-52b2-809c-06d7c4b4e286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a65798db-1640-5ac0-9550-f0316b86d818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289133} :answer-field/id #uuid "827ea8a6-3f45-58a9-acce-ef8eb4a48299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290018}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4b49482-74ae-5d27-81da-2160ac3cb246" :question/math-academy-id "q-2195" :question/problem "$\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $2x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{13}}{13}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e9f2b0-37e6-50e9-81f9-f4870eb89a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244649} {:answer/id #uuid "5c181d1d-bf18-55ce-a822-d1dd57423b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245123} {:answer/id #uuid "96815189-eaa4-553f-a459-50b35808ed3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245641} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1a82cf-c0af-5ed8-82c6-891e0d588025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245732} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcc076d-df76-5525-b8c1-56042f929b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c181d1d-bf18-55ce-a822-d1dd57423b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245123} :answer-field/id #uuid "393c10c8-dc0a-514f-91a5-4a6f69993612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246644}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fab21e9-9cee-52c0-ac1e-55235a31831f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113581" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $12{x}^{5}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2{x}^{6}-3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =12{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\cdot 12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2{x}^{6}-3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2029e990-e06c-5de3-bbb1-b0f88ba76514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288239} {:answer/id #uuid "26e955b2-8d3d-529c-aa79-f141fc091ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288288} {:answer/id #uuid "2df24f84-49a3-5635-8292-77d1429d86ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288342} {:answer/id #uuid "836b0c8d-6627-5e01-a339-6971dc4ede71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5{x}^{2}-4)^{2/3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288915} {:answer/id #uuid "b431a516-679a-55de-bc9a-2dca5dba0032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(5{x}^{2}-4)^{2/3}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26e955b2-8d3d-529c-aa79-f141fc091ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288288} :answer-field/id #uuid "8489e6ea-2848-5483-8e22-602ea51d9d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290022}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f908f52d-efaa-5ed9-92ad-fd2900015e8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-2390" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $10x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=5{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=10x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,10x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=5{x}^{2}-4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181818f9-ee5d-570b-8805-3453bec6da26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336257} {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} {:answer/id #uuid "601c5f25-3385-59f3-b9ec-6f4783410687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336901} {:answer/id #uuid "d13e9d9a-a682-5a93-b274-9657dd12c702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337864} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d6ab9a-d942-55d2-8674-766bbfa15318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} :answer-field/id #uuid "1287170e-aae1-5d7e-9ef3-ec229bb6713f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338307}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94a2c36c-7f20-5b89-950f-742f856d9127" :question/math-academy-id "q-72382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $5{x}^{4}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the radical. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{1/2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{5}$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 1